  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The dataset titled \"Choanoeca flexa transcriptome and predicted nonredundant proteome,\" made accessible in 2019, presents a valuable resource for researchers working in computational biology and genetics, although it has not yet garnered citations or wide public mention. Despite its limited visibility, it has seen some reuse in biomedical literature, indicating its potential for contributing to ongoing research in related fields.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This dataset, authored by Thibaut Brunet, includes a comprehensive transcriptome assembly and a predicted proteome for the organism Choanoeca flexa. The accessibility of the data underpins current open access principles and aims to facilitate further research in various domains, including bioinformatics, gene expression, and genetics. As an open-access dataset, it allows researchers across disciplines to explore and utilize the genetic information presented without the barriers associated with traditional publication formats.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Although the dataset has not yet received any formal citations or mentions in significant online forums, it serves as an important reference point for researchers in computational biology looking to enhance their understanding of Choanoeca flexa or related species. Its inclusion in a reputable repository like Figshare underscores its academic significance and credibility.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The dataset has demonstrated some downstream reuse, having been referenced in a biomedical article available through Europe PMC. This suggests that while initial engagement may be modest, the work contributes to a growing body of knowledge and may inspire further studies, especially in the fields of molecular biology and genetic research.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">In summary, the \"Choanoeca flexa transcriptome and predicted nonredundant proteome\" dataset is positioned to play a crucial role in advancing research in its relevant fields despite currently low visibility. Its commitment to open data practices not only supports ongoing research initiatives but also aligns with broader trends in the scientific community towards transparency and accessibility in scholarly work.</p>",
